    "detailed_answer": "The Isle of Wight became separated from mainland Britain approximately 7,000 years ago during the Holocene period. This separation occurred due to rising sea levels following the last Ice Age, when the English Channel was formed and the Solent (the strait separating the Isle of Wight from mainland England) was created. The island's geological formation dates back much further, with its oldest rocks forming about 110 million years ago, but the actual separation from the mainland occurred around 7,000 years ago as sea levels rose and flooded the land connection."
