{
    "short_answer": "An object that moves around an external axis is said to be in revolution or orbit.",
    "detailed_answer": "In physics, when an object moves around an external axis (an axis completely outside the object itself), this type of motion is called revolution or orbit. For example, the Earth revolving around the Sun demonstrates this concept, where the Sun serves as the external axis of rotation. This differs from rotation, which occurs when an object spins around an internal axis within the object itself. Sources: Standard physics terminology and educational resources define revolution as motion around an external axis."
}

                           {
    "short_answer": "Rib vaults consist of diagonal ribs, transverse arches, wall ribs, ridge ribs, and webbing.",
    "detailed_answer": "Rib vaults are composed of several key architectural elements: diagonal ribs that cross diagonally across the vault, transverse arches that run perpendicular to the nave axis, wall ribs (or formerets) along the walls, ridge ribs along the crown of the vault, and webbing (the masonry infill between the ribs). At Wells Cathedral, these elements created the distinctive 'net vaults' that anticipated later Gothic developments. The diagonal ribs were typically semicircular arches that raised the vault center above the level of transverse arches and wall ribs. This rib framework supported the webbing and allowed for larger windows and more complex vaulting patterns characteristic of Gothic architecture."
}

